WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.020
In the two decades after World War Two, the
United States almost doubled Federal investment

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.260
in scientific research and technological development.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.660
And it tells the story of a literal moonshot.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.280
Here in 1957, the Soviet Union launched into
space Sputnik

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.360
the first man-made satellite.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.860
It marked the start of the space race between
the U.S. and Soviets

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.560
ushering in the space age.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.560
"Today a new moon is in the sky.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.380
A 23-inch metal sphere placed in orbit by a Russian rocket.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.540
One of the great scientific feats of the age."

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.500
After that, Federal funding into science,
technology, and military development

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.400
went into hyperdrive.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:43.560
A year later, Congress created the
NASA space agency.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:49.460
That same year President Eisenhower established
ARPA, the Advanced Research Projects Agency,

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.640
to develop space technology for military use.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.680
Today we know it as DARPA.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.860
Funding flowed easily, often through universities,
into all aspects of R&amp;D.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.800
And in 1969, it paid off.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.900
That year, with the world watching, the Apollo
11 space mission

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.980
landed the first humans on the moon.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.500
"Liftoff! We have a liftoff!"

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.660
"That's one small step for a man.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.520
One giant leap for mankind."

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.840
Beating the Soviets to the moon was the goal,
to be sure.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.340
But the ingenuity and discovery fostered during
this period helped the U.S. achieve economic

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.100
and military superiority,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.400
funding innovations that are at the foundation
of our modern lives.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.860
Take your smartphone.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:42.860
What would it be without innovations like
touchscreens, GPS, and the internet?

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.120
These were all technologies supported by your
taxpayer dollars.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.800
Case in point, remember that obscure agency
called ARPA?

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.160
Well, the same year Neil Armstrong set foot
on the moon,

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.100
researchers over at ARPA connected a network of four computers.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.720
They called it ARPANET.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.140
Today, we call it the internet.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:06.320
Yet, federal investment
dropped from its peak of almost 2%

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.200
to just 7% of GDP.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.040
At the same time, private industry investment
in R&amp;D grew dramatically.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.520
Eventually reaching a level twice that of
public funding.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.360
In fact, as a percent of GDP, the United States
is at one of its lowest levels of public funding

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.860
since before World War Two.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.180
Now you might look at these numbers and say,
"So what?

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.980
Companies are making the investments instead
of hard-working taxpayers."

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.280
And while you’re right, it does pose a problem.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.000
Companies investing in new technology is a
good thing.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.180
But the nature of public R&amp;D funding is different
than that of private.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.780
Public funds can go into early-stage, longer-term,
and often riskier endeavors.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:54.300
Projects that may not pay off anytime soon,
but that could be the next moonshot.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.660
This adds up to another big problem when we
look at international competition--

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.820
especially the rise of China.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:06.420
Over the past two decades, China has increased
its R&amp;D spending more than tenfold

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.379
In that same period, U.S. spending has remained
flat.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:16.580
What’s worse: China is projected to overtake
the U.S. in total spending by 2029.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.160
Just ten short years away.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.980
And that's the problem.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:26.820
In a time of rapid technological change, the
U.S. is on the brink of ceding jobs, profit,

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.280
and overall economic growth to its largest
competitor.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:35.200
China’s government-led plans to become the world leader in cutting-edge technologies

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.760
are already paying off.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.400
They’re submitting more patent applications,
publishing more scientific papers,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.180
and producing more STEM graduates than any other country.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.420
That means that Chinese companies could soon
dominate emerging technologies

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:54.760
like artificial intelligence, robotics, quantum computing,
synthetic biology,

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.660
and next generation communication technologies like 5G.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:03.560
That, in turn, would give the Chinese military
a big boost, putting the Pentagon

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.880
at serious risk of losing its strategic advantage.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.080
Think back to the 50s and 60s.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.780
What if those moonshots didn't happen because
the investment wasn't there?

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:16.640
Or worse yet, another country got there first?

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.920
How much would U.S. competitiveness have suffered?

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.060
Well, that future may soon be a reality.

